Chapter: RepoProtocolPersist
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Parse
  cites Foreword chapter TextSearch
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter ImportGate
  cites Foreword chapter SourceDefWire
  cites Encode chapter Hex
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  cites Kernel chapter AppPersist
  cites Works chapter RepoProtocol
  cites Works chapter KeyManager
  cites Works chapter Annotation
  cites Works chapter Discussion

 Persistent storage for the Codex repository protocol. Nine
 fact kinds (30-38) are serialized as text and stored via DiskFacts,
 following the ServicesPersist pattern.

 The header fields of every kind are pipe-delimited, and a pipe cannot appear
 in one of them. Kind 30 is the exception, and it has to be: a source
 definition now carries the source, and a pipe is how this language writes a
 sum type. Its content is the last field, length-prefixed, and it is never
 split. See Definition Persistence below.

Section: Kind Constants

  persist-kind-definition : Integer = 30
  persist-kind-proof : Integer = 31
  persist-kind-vouch : Integer = 32
  persist-kind-policy : Integer = 33
  persist-kind-capability : Integer = 34
  persist-kind-annotation : Integer = 35
  persist-kind-proposal : Integer = 36
  persist-kind-verdict : Integer = 37
  persist-kind-discussion : Integer = 38
  persist-kind-tombstone : Integer = 39
  persist-kind-index-snapshot : Integer = 40

Section: Record Types

 New record types for fact kinds that lack existing application
 types. Kinds 35-38 serialize existing types from Annotation,
 RepoProtocol, and Discussion.

 A SourceDefinition used to be a POINTER: a hash, and a Perforce path where
 the bytes could be found. That is an index over somebody else's store, not a
 store, and it is the whole of the self-hosted repository gap -- the founding
 document proposes
 deleting GitHub, and we had not deleted Perforce.

 So it carries the bytes now. sd-content is the source itself; sd-author and
 sd-timestamp are the two things fact-signing-content covers that the hash
 does not reach, and without them a definition read back off the disk cannot
 be turned into the SignedFact the import gate verifies.

  SourceDefinition = record {
    sd-hash : Text,
    sd-path : Text,
    sd-quire : Text,
    sd-chapter : Text,
    sd-signer : Text,
    sd-signature : List Integer,
    sd-author : Text,
    sd-timestamp : Integer,
    sd-content : Text
  }

  RepoProof = record {
    rp-hash : Text,
    rp-claim : Text,
    rp-evidence : Text,
    rp-author : Text,
    rp-timestamp : Integer
  }

  RepoVouch = record {
    rv-voucher : Text,
    rv-subject : Text,
    rv-scope : Text,
    rv-trust-level : Integer,
    rv-timestamp : Integer
  }

  RepoPolicy = record {
    rpy-name : Text,
    rpy-target : Text,
    rpy-rule : Text,
    rpy-author : Text,
    rpy-timestamp : Integer
  }

  RepoCap = record {
    rc-name : Text,
    rc-holder : Text,
    rc-permissions : Text,
    rc-expiry : Integer,
    rc-timestamp : Integer
  }

Section: Helpers

  join-with-comma : List Text, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  join-with-comma (items) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let sep = if i == 0 then "" else ","
    in join-with-comma items (i + 1) len (acc & sep & list-at items i)

  split-comma : Text -> List Text
  split-comma (txt) =
    if text-length txt == 0 then []
    else text-split txt ","

  parse-annotation-kind : Text -> AnnotationKind
  parse-annotation-kind (s) =
    if s == "rationale" then Rationale
    else if s == "invariant" then Invariant
    else if s == "warning" then Warning
    else if s == "discovery" then Discovery
    else if s == "doctrine" then Doctrine
    else Todo

  proposal-status-to-int : ProposalStatus -> Integer
  proposal-status-to-int (s) = when s
    is ProposalOpen -> 0
    is ProposalAccepted -> 1
    is ProposalRejected -> 2
    is ProposalAmended -> 3

  int-to-proposal-status : Integer -> ProposalStatus
  int-to-proposal-status (n) =
    if n == 0 then ProposalOpen
    else if n == 1 then ProposalAccepted
    else if n == 2 then ProposalRejected
    else ProposalAmended

  verdict-kind-to-int : VerdictKind -> Integer
  verdict-kind-to-int (k) = when k
    is Accept -> 0
    is Reject -> 1
    is Amend (note) -> 2
    is Abstain -> 3

Section: Definition Persistence (Kind 30)

 Format: hash|path|quire|chapter|signer-fp|sig-hex|author|timestamp|len|<content>

 The nine header fields are pipe-delimited and none of them can contain a
 pipe. The content can, and does: a pipe is how this language writes a sum
 type, so nearly every chapter in the tree has one. Content is therefore the
 LAST field, it is length-prefixed, and it is never split -- the reader finds
 the ninth pipe and takes the next `len` characters verbatim, whatever they
 are. Splitting the whole line on "|" and hoping, which is what this chapter
 used to do to every field it had, would cut a source file to pieces at its
 own type declarations.

 A line with no ninth pipe is one of the old pointer-shaped records: it parses,
 and it yields empty content. Reading an old store must not fail; it must
 tell you honestly that the bytes were never there.

 The wire format itself -- the nine fields, the ninth-pipe rule, the
 length-prefixed content, the signature's hex -- lives in Foreword chapter
 SourceDefWire, so that the compiler's reader (FactDisk) and this writer
 decode one format and not two. This chapter maps its own SourceDefinition
 to and from that wire record; the legacy pointer-shaped records, which are
 this store's history and not the wire format, stay here.

  def-to-wire : SourceDefinition -> SourceDefWire
  def-to-wire (d) =
    SourceDefWire {
      sdw-hash = d.sd-hash, sdw-path = d.sd-path, sdw-quire = d.sd-quire,
      sdw-chapter = d.sd-chapter, sdw-signer = d.sd-signer,
      sdw-signature = d.sd-signature, sdw-author = d.sd-author,
      sdw-timestamp = d.sd-timestamp, sdw-content = d.sd-content
    }

  def-from-wire : SourceDefWire -> SourceDefinition
  def-from-wire (w) =
    SourceDefinition {
      sd-hash = w.sdw-hash, sd-path = w.sdw-path, sd-quire = w.sdw-quire,
      sd-chapter = w.sdw-chapter, sd-signer = w.sdw-signer,
      sd-signature = w.sdw-signature, sd-author = w.sdw-author,
      sd-timestamp = w.sdw-timestamp, sd-content = w.sdw-content
    }

  source-def-to-text : SourceDefinition -> Maybe Text
  source-def-to-text (d) = sdw-encode (def-to-wire d)

  source-def-from-text : Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  source-def-from-text (line) =
    when sdw-decode line
      is Just (w) -> Just (def-from-wire w)
      is None -> source-def-from-legacy line

  source-def-from-legacy : Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  source-def-from-legacy (line) =
    let parts = text-split line "|"
    in if list-length parts < 4 then None
    else let signer = if list-length parts >= 6 then list-at parts 4 else ""
    in let sig = if list-length parts >= 6 then hex-decode (list-at parts 5) else []
    in Just (SourceDefinition {
      sd-hash = list-at parts 0,
      sd-path = list-at parts 1,
      sd-quire = list-at parts 2,
      sd-chapter = list-at parts 3,
      sd-signer = signer,
      sd-signature = sig,
      sd-author = "",
      sd-timestamp = 0,
      sd-content = ""
    })

Section: A Definition Is A Signed Fact

 The gate in Foreword chapter ImportGate takes a FactBundle and a
 TrustManifest and answers with one of seven outcomes. It has always been able
 to; nothing could ever reach it from the disk, because a persisted definition
 was not a Fact and its signature did not cover one. sign-definition signed the
 string "hash|path|quire|chapter" -- a sentence the gate has never heard of --
 so a stored definition could not have passed gate-import even in principle.

 These three functions are the join. A SourceDefinition becomes the Fact its
 hash addresses, wearing the signature the gate checks, filed in a bundle the
 gate can read.

  source-def-fact : SourceDefinition -> Fact
  source-def-fact (d) =
    Fact {
      hash = d.sd-hash,
      kind = DefinitionFact,
      content = d.sd-content,
      author = d.sd-author,
      timestamp = d.sd-timestamp
    }

  source-def-signed : SourceDefinition -> SignedFact
  source-def-signed (d) =
    SignedFact {
      fact = source-def-fact d,
      signature = d.sd-signature,
      signer-fingerprint = d.sd-signer
    }

  bundle-from-definitions : List SourceDefinition -> FactBundle
  bundle-from-definitions (defs) =
    bundle-from-defs-loop defs 0 (list-length defs) bundle-empty

  bundle-from-defs-loop : List SourceDefinition, Integer, Integer, FactBundle -> FactBundle
  bundle-from-defs-loop (defs) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let d = list-at defs i
    in let next = if list-length (d.sd-signature) == 0
      then bundle-offer acc (source-def-fact d)
      else bundle-publish acc (source-def-signed d)
    in bundle-from-defs-loop defs (i + 1) len next

 The whole point, in one function: everything the disk holds, offered to the
 gate.

  repo-bundle-from-disk : DiskFactStore -> FactBundle
  repo-bundle-from-disk (store) =
    bundle-from-definitions (repo-persist-load-all-definitions store)

Section: An Index Of Works

 The store could not answer the two questions anybody actually asks it.

 GIVE ME Colophon.codex. It had no way to take a name. app-scan-last takes a
 KIND, so it returned the last fact of kind 30 -- the last source file written,
 whichever chapter that happened to be -- and asked for one chapter it would
 hand you another. It did not fail; it answered the wrong question, which is
 worse.

 WHICH VERSION IS THE CURRENT ONE. Two editions of a file are two facts, both
 signed, both admitted, and nothing on the disk preferred either.

 Neither of those needs a supersession record to fix, and I spent a while
 believing they did. What they need is a KEY. A source definition already has
 one -- its path -- and once a fact has a key the log means what every
 log-structured store has always meant by it: the last write for a key is the
 current value of that key. Last-write-wins is not a compromise here, it is the
 semantics; git does it with refs and an LSM tree does it with levels.

 So the index is built by walking the definitions in LOG ORDER and letting
 later writes overwrite earlier ones. Two things fall out, and they are the two
 halves of what a repository is:

   wi-current maps a path to the hash of its current content. That is the tree.
   wi-works maps a hash to the work. That is the history, and it is complete,
   because a content-addressed store cannot lose an edition it was given.

 Superseding is not deleting. The first edition of Colophon.codex is still in
 wi-works and still verifies, forever; it is simply no longer what the path
 points at. Removal is a separate act, and it wants a tombstone: see
 Authenticated Removal below, where it is built.

 This is also the reason DiskFacts' own in-memory index cannot serve: disk-load
 keys each fact by the hash of the stored LINE, not by the hash of the content
 that line describes. It is an index of records. This is an index of works.

  WorkIndex = record {
    wi-current : HamtMap Text,
    wi-works : HamtMap SourceDefinition,
    wi-paths : List Text
  }

  work-index-empty : WorkIndex
  work-index-empty = WorkIndex {
    wi-current = hamt-empty,
    wi-works = hamt-empty,
    wi-paths = []
  }

 Superseding was never deleting, and neither is removal: a tombstone does
 not erase a fact, it records that a PATH is gone as of a point in the log.
 So the index reads definitions and tombstones together, in log order, and
 the last word for a path wins -- a definition puts the path in the tree, a
 later tombstone takes it out, a later definition puts it back. What none of
 this touches is wi-works: every edition ever stored is still addressable by
 its hash and still verifies, forever, tombstone or no. The tree forgets a
 path; the history does not forget a work.

  repo-index-from-disk : DiskFactStore -> WorkIndex
  repo-index-from-disk (store) =
    let entries = app-scan-two-kinds store persist-kind-definition persist-kind-tombstone
    in index-entries entries 0 (list-length entries) work-index-empty

  index-entries : List KindText, Integer, Integer, WorkIndex -> WorkIndex
  index-entries (entries) (i) (len) (ix) =
    if i >= len then ix
    else let e = list-at entries i
    in let ix2 = if e.kt-kind == persist-kind-tombstone
       then index-remove ix (tombstone-path (e.kt-text))
       else index-add ix (e.kt-text)
    in index-entries entries (i + 1) len ix2

  index-add : WorkIndex, Text -> WorkIndex
  index-add (ix) (text) =
    when source-def-from-text text
      is None -> ix
      is Just (d) -> index-one ix d

 A tombstone's content is the path it retires. The path leaves wi-current
 (so no version is current) and leaves wi-paths (so a checkout does not
 walk it); wi-works is untouched.

  index-remove : WorkIndex, Text -> WorkIndex
  index-remove (ix) (path) =
    WorkIndex {
      wi-current = hamt-remove (ix.wi-current) path,
      wi-works = ix.wi-works,
      wi-paths = paths-without (ix.wi-paths) path
    }

  paths-without : List Text, Text -> List Text
  paths-without (paths) (path) = paths-without-loop paths path 0 (list-length paths) []

  paths-without-loop : List Text, Text, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  paths-without-loop (paths) (path) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let p = list-at paths i
    in if p == path then paths-without-loop paths path (i + 1) len acc
       else paths-without-loop paths path (i + 1) len (list-push acc p)

  index-one : WorkIndex, SourceDefinition -> WorkIndex
  index-one (ix) (d) =
    WorkIndex {
      wi-current = hamt-set (ix.wi-current) (d.sd-path) (d.sd-hash),
      wi-works = hamt-set (ix.wi-works) (d.sd-hash) d,
      wi-paths = if path-known ix (d.sd-path) then ix.wi-paths
                 else list-push (ix.wi-paths) (d.sd-path)
    }

 index-one in two halves, for a reader that must record an edition in the
 history without letting it decide the tree. index-works-add files the work by
 its hash and touches nothing else; index-tree-set points a path at a work and
 leaves the history alone.

  index-works-add : WorkIndex, SourceDefinition -> WorkIndex
  index-works-add (ix) (d) =
    WorkIndex {
      wi-current = ix.wi-current,
      wi-works = hamt-set (ix.wi-works) (d.sd-hash) d,
      wi-paths = ix.wi-paths
    }

  index-tree-set : WorkIndex, SourceDefinition -> WorkIndex
  index-tree-set (ix) (d) =
    WorkIndex {
      wi-current = hamt-set (ix.wi-current) (d.sd-path) (d.sd-hash),
      wi-works = ix.wi-works,
      wi-paths = if path-known ix (d.sd-path) then ix.wi-paths
                 else list-push (ix.wi-paths) (d.sd-path)
    }

  path-known : WorkIndex, Text -> Boolean
  path-known (ix) (path) =
    when hamt-get (ix.wi-current) path
      is Just (h) -> True
      is None -> False

Section: Asking The Index

 Give me the current version of this path.

  work-current : WorkIndex, Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  work-current (ix) (path) =
    when hamt-get (ix.wi-current) path
      is None -> None
      is Just (h) -> hamt-get (ix.wi-works) h

 Give me this exact work, whatever the tree says today. A hash is a name that
 cannot go stale, which is the whole of why we address by them.

  work-by-hash : WorkIndex, Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  work-by-hash (ix) (h) = hamt-get (ix.wi-works) h

 Every path the store knows about, in the order it first heard of them. This is
 the tree, and it is the thing a checkout walks.

  work-tree : WorkIndex -> List Text
  work-tree (ix) = ix.wi-paths

Section: Checkout

 A checkout is the whole of what a repository is for. Everything else -- the
 hashes, the signatures, the gate, the log -- exists so that this one function
 can hand you the source back and you can believe it.

 And it is a walk of the tree fetching each current work, which is exactly as
 dull as it should be. The interesting properties are not in this code, they
 are underneath it: the bytes came back byte-identical because they were
 content-addressed, the revised edition was taken and the superseded one left
 because the last write for a path is the current value of that path, and the
 whole thing verified because every work carries a signature the gate checks.
 A checkout that had to be clever would mean something below it was wrong.

 The result is the concatenation the compiler eats. That is not an accident of
 convenience: the point of it is that a program checked out of the store can be
 compiled and run, and the first one was.

  repo-checkout : WorkIndex -> Text
  repo-checkout (ix) =
    let paths = work-tree ix
    in checkout-loop ix paths 0 (list-length paths) ""

  checkout-loop : WorkIndex, List Text, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  checkout-loop (ix) (paths) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let p = list-at paths i
    in when work-current ix p
      is None -> checkout-loop ix paths (i + 1) len acc
      is Just (d) -> checkout-loop ix paths (i + 1) len (acc & (d.sd-content))

 One work, checked out on its own. A checkout of the whole tree is this in a
 loop, and a checkout of nothing is honestly empty rather than absent.

  work-content : WorkIndex, Text -> Text
  work-content (ix) (path) =
    when work-current ix path
      is None -> ""
      is Just (d) -> d.sd-content

Section: Persisting The Index

 repo-index-from-disk reads every definition and tombstone the log holds and
 folds them, in order, into the tree and the history. It is correct and it is
 O(log): a store that has recorded a thousand editions replays a thousand
 editions on every boot, whether or not any of them is still current.

 A snapshot is the way out. It is one fact -- kind 40 -- carrying the whole
 materialized WorkIndex as of a point in the log: the tree (each live path and
 its current hash) and the history (every edition ever stored, so that a hash
 that is no longer current is still addressable, forever, which is the one
 property this store cannot lose). Rebuilding then means loading the snapshot
 and replaying only the entries written after it -- the tail -- instead of the
 whole log.

 Two things key a snapshot, and both are in the superblock. The COVERED HEAD is
 the log-head the snapshot was taken at; the tail is everything from there to
 the current head. The INDEX-GEN is bumped by compaction, which rewrites the
 log and moves every sector -- so a snapshot whose gen no longer matches the
 store describes sector positions that no longer mean what they did, and the
 rebuild falls back to a full replay rather than trust it. A snapshot is a
 cache that a full replay can always reconstruct; it is never the only copy of
 anything, because every fact it summarizes is still on the disk.

 The format is a header of four integers -- gen, covered-head, path-count,
 work-count -- then the body. The body is length-prefixed segments so that a
 path, a hash, or a source file full of pipes and newlines is taken verbatim by
 its length and never parsed for delimiters: first each (path, current-hash)
 pair, then each work as its SourceDefWire text. A segment is its character
 length, a pipe, then exactly that many characters.

  IntRead = record { ir-value : Integer, ir-next : Integer }
  SegRead = record { sr-value : Text, sr-next : Integer }
  ReadProg = record { rp-next : Integer, rp-index : WorkIndex }
  SnapIndex = record { si-gen : Integer, si-head : Integer, si-index : WorkIndex }

  snap-pipe-code : Integer = char-code (char-at "|" 0)

  snap-index-of-pipe : Text, Integer -> Integer
  snap-index-of-pipe (s) (i) =
    if i >= text-length s then i
    else if char-code (char-at s i) == snap-pipe-code then i
    else snap-index-of-pipe s (i + 1)

  snap-read-int : Text, Integer -> IntRead
  snap-read-int (s) (off) =
    let p = snap-index-of-pipe s off
    in IntRead { ir-value = text-to-integer (substring s off (p - off)), ir-next = p + 1 }

  seg-put : Text -> Text
  seg-put (s) = show (text-length s) & "|" & s

  snap-read-seg : Text, Integer -> SegRead
  snap-read-seg (s) (off) =
    let ir = snap-read-int s off
    in SegRead { sr-value = substring s (ir.ir-next) (ir.ir-value), sr-next = ir.ir-next + ir.ir-value }

 The whole materialized index as one text. The works come out of wi-works in no
 particular order, which is fine -- they are keyed by hash and the tree carries
 the order that matters.

  work-list : WorkIndex -> List SourceDefinition
  work-list (ix) = hamt-fold (\acc -> \k -> \d -> list-push acc d) [] (ix.wi-works)

  snap-encode : WorkIndex, Integer, Integer -> Text
  snap-encode (ix) (gen) (head) =
    let paths = ix.wi-paths
    in let works = work-list ix
    in show gen & "|" & show head & "|" & show (list-length paths) & "|" & show (list-length works) & "|"
       & snap-paths-enc ix paths 0 (list-length paths) ""
       & snap-works-enc works 0 (list-length works) ""

  snap-paths-enc : WorkIndex, List Text, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  snap-paths-enc (ix) (paths) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let p = list-at paths i
    in let h = when hamt-get (ix.wi-current) p is Just (hh) -> hh is None -> ""
    in snap-paths-enc ix paths (i + 1) len (acc & seg-put p & seg-put h)

  snap-works-enc : List SourceDefinition, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  snap-works-enc (works) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let enc = when source-def-to-text (list-at works i) is Just (t) -> seg-put t is None -> ""
    in snap-works-enc works (i + 1) len (acc & enc)

 The generation, read on its own and cheaply, so a rebuild can decide whether a
 snapshot is worth parsing before it parses the whole thing.

  snap-gen : Text -> Integer
  snap-gen (s) = (snap-read-int s 0).ir-value

  snap-to-index : Text -> SnapIndex
  snap-to-index (s) =
    let ig = snap-read-int s 0
    in let ih = snap-read-int s (ig.ir-next)
    in let inp = snap-read-int s (ih.ir-next)
    in let inw = snap-read-int s (inp.ir-next)
    in let after-paths = snap-read-paths s (inw.ir-next) (inp.ir-value) work-index-empty
    in let after-works = snap-read-works s (after-paths.rp-next) (inw.ir-value) (after-paths.rp-index)
    in SnapIndex { si-gen = ig.ir-value, si-head = ih.ir-value, si-index = after-works.rp-index }

  snap-read-paths : Text, Integer, Integer, WorkIndex -> ReadProg
  snap-read-paths (s) (off) (remaining) (ix) =
    if remaining <= 0 then ReadProg { rp-next = off, rp-index = ix }
    else let ps = snap-read-seg s off
    in let hs = snap-read-seg s (ps.sr-next)
    in snap-read-paths s (hs.sr-next) (remaining - 1) (snap-put-current ix (ps.sr-value) (hs.sr-value))

  snap-put-current : WorkIndex, Text, Text -> WorkIndex
  snap-put-current (ix) (path) (hash) =
    WorkIndex {
      wi-current = hamt-set (ix.wi-current) path hash,
      wi-works = ix.wi-works,
      wi-paths = list-push (ix.wi-paths) path
    }

  snap-read-works : Text, Integer, Integer, WorkIndex -> ReadProg
  snap-read-works (s) (off) (remaining) (ix) =
    if remaining <= 0 then ReadProg { rp-next = off, rp-index = ix }
    else let ws = snap-read-seg s off
    in snap-read-works s (ws.sr-next) (remaining - 1) (snap-put-work ix (ws.sr-value))

  snap-put-work : WorkIndex, Text -> WorkIndex
  snap-put-work (ix) (text) =
    when source-def-from-text text
      is None -> ix
      is Just (d) -> WorkIndex {
        wi-current = ix.wi-current,
        wi-works = hamt-set (ix.wi-works) (d.sd-hash) d,
        wi-paths = ix.wi-paths
      }

 Take a snapshot of the index as it stands. The covered head is the current
 log-head -- everything on the disk right now -- captured before the snapshot
 fact is written, so the snapshot describes the log up to but not including
 itself. The kind-40 fact is not a definition or a tombstone, so a later
 rebuild's tail walk steps over it and folds only the real entries after it.

  repo-snapshot-save : DiskFactStore, WorkIndex, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  repo-snapshot-save (store) (ix) (timestamp) =
    let sb = store.superblock
    in app-write-and-checkpoint store persist-kind-index-snapshot (snap-encode ix (sb.index-gen) (sb.log-head)) timestamp

 The fast rebuild. With a snapshot whose generation still matches the store,
 load it and replay the tail; with none, or one stranded by a compaction, fall
 back to the full replay, which is always correct. The result is the same
 WorkIndex either way -- a snapshot is an optimization, never a source of
 truth.

  repo-index-build : DiskFactStore -> WorkIndex
  repo-index-build (store) =
    when app-scan-last store persist-kind-index-snapshot
      is None -> repo-index-from-disk store
      is Just (snaptext) ->
        if snap-gen snaptext == store.superblock.index-gen
        then let snap = snap-to-index snaptext
        in repo-index-replay-tail store (snap.si-head) (snap.si-index)
        else repo-index-from-disk store

  repo-index-replay-tail : DiskFactStore, Integer, WorkIndex -> WorkIndex
  repo-index-replay-tail (store) (from-sector) (ix) =
    let entries = app-scan-two-kinds-from store from-sector persist-kind-definition persist-kind-tombstone
    in index-entries entries 0 (list-length entries) ix

Section: Authenticated Removal

 A definition is signed, and the gate can refuse one it does not trust. A
 tombstone was not: its content was the bare path it retired, so anyone who
 could write a sector could retire any path, and the tree honored it. Removal
 is an assertion like any other, and an unauthenticated assertion is exactly
 the hole the store exists to close.

 So a tombstone carries a signature now. It signs the path together with the
 author and the timestamp -- the same three things a definition's signature
 binds beyond its content hash -- so neither the path being retired nor its
 attribution can be altered without breaking the signature. The wire form is
 five pipe-delimited fields, path first; a line with fewer fields is a bare
 legacy tombstone, read as an unsigned removal of that path.

 Two indexes read the same log. repo-index-from-disk is TRUSTING: it honors
 every tombstone by its path, signed or not, resolving each path by log order,
 which is what the existing tree has always done and what the fast rebuild
 inherits. repo-index-verified is AUTHENTICATED, and it does two things the
 trusting index does not.

 First, it VERIFIES every assertion. A definition must hash to its own hash and
 carry a signature that verifies against a key the manifest holds at or above
 the threshold -- the same check the gate applies; a tombstone must verify the
 same way. An unsigned, forged, or untrusted definition or tombstone is dropped
 on the floor. Every verified edition still enters the history (addressable by
 hash forever); only verified assertions touch the tree.

 Second, it resolves each path by the SIGNED TIMESTAMP, not by log position.
 This is what closes REPLAY. A tombstone or a definition is signed together
 with its timestamp, so an attacker who can append to the log can copy an old
 assertion forward but cannot forge a newer one. The tree records, per path,
 the timestamp of the assertion currently deciding it, and an assertion changes
 a path only when its timestamp strictly exceeds that -- so a replayed old
 tombstone cannot retire a path a newer definition has re-added, and a replayed
 old definition cannot resurrect a path a newer tombstone has retired. Log
 order decides nothing an attacker could exploit; only the signatures do.

  Tombstone = record {
    tomb-path : Text,
    tomb-signer : Text,
    tomb-signature : List Integer,
    tomb-author : Text,
    tomb-timestamp : Integer
  }

  tombstone-signing-content : Text, Text, Integer -> Text
  tombstone-signing-content (path) (author) (timestamp) =
    path & "|" & author & "|" & show timestamp

  sign-tombstone : Keypair, Text, Integer -> Tombstone
  sign-tombstone (kp) (path) (timestamp) =
    Tombstone {
      tomb-path = path,
      tomb-signer = kp.fingerprint,
      tomb-signature = sign-content kp (tombstone-signing-content path (kp.fingerprint) timestamp),
      tomb-author = kp.fingerprint,
      tomb-timestamp = timestamp
    }

  tombstone-to-text : Tombstone -> Text
  tombstone-to-text (t) =
    t.tomb-path & "|" & t.tomb-signer & "|" & bytes-to-hex (t.tomb-signature)
    & "|" & t.tomb-author & "|" & show (t.tomb-timestamp)

  tombstone-from-text : Text -> Tombstone
  tombstone-from-text (line) =
    let parts = text-split line "|"
    in if list-length parts >= 5
      then Tombstone {
        tomb-path = list-at parts 0,
        tomb-signer = list-at parts 1,
        tomb-signature = hex-decode (list-at parts 2),
        tomb-author = list-at parts 3,
        tomb-timestamp = parse-decimal-full (list-at parts 4)
      }
      else Tombstone {
        tomb-path = line, tomb-signer = "", tomb-signature = [],
        tomb-author = "", tomb-timestamp = 0
      }

  tombstone-path : Text -> Text
  tombstone-path (line) = (tombstone-from-text line).tomb-path

  tombstone-verify : Tombstone, TrustManifest, Integer -> Boolean
  tombstone-verify (t) (manifest) (threshold) =
    when manifest-lookup manifest (t.tomb-signer)
      is None -> False
      is Just (tk) -> if tk.score >= threshold
        then verify-content-signature (tk.public-key) (tombstone-signing-content (t.tomb-path) (t.tomb-author) (t.tomb-timestamp)) (t.tomb-signature)
        else False

  repo-persist-save-tombstone-signed : DiskFactStore, Keypair, Text, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  repo-persist-save-tombstone-signed (store) (kp) (path) (timestamp) =
    app-write-and-checkpoint store persist-kind-tombstone (tombstone-to-text (sign-tombstone kp path timestamp)) timestamp

 A definition verifies exactly as the gate verifies it: its content must hash
 to the hash it claims, and that hash, kind, author and timestamp must carry a
 signature from a key the manifest trusts at or above the threshold.

  def-verify : SourceDefinition, TrustManifest, Integer -> Boolean
  def-verify (d) (manifest) (threshold) =
    if fact-content-hash (d.sd-content) == d.sd-hash
    then when manifest-lookup manifest (d.sd-signer)
      is None -> False
      is Just (tk) -> if tk.score >= threshold
        then verify-fact-signature (tk.public-key) (source-def-signed d)
        else False
    else False

 The authenticated build carries, beside the index, a stamp per path: the
 timestamp of the assertion currently deciding that path. An assertion -- a
 definition or a tombstone -- moves a path only when its own timestamp strictly
 exceeds the stamp, so nothing an attacker replays out of the log can override
 a newer signature. A path with no stamp yet is decided by the first verified
 assertion to name it.

  VerifiedAcc = record {
    va-index : WorkIndex,
    va-stamp : HamtMap Integer
  }

  verified-acc-empty : VerifiedAcc = VerifiedAcc { va-index = work-index-empty, va-stamp = hamt-empty }

  stamp-wins : HamtMap Integer, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  stamp-wins (stamp) (path) (ts) =
    when hamt-get stamp path
      is None -> True
      is Just (cur) -> ts > cur

  repo-index-verified : DiskFactStore, TrustManifest, Integer -> WorkIndex
  repo-index-verified (store) (manifest) (threshold) =
    let entries = app-scan-two-kinds store persist-kind-definition persist-kind-tombstone
    in (vindex-entries entries 0 (list-length entries) verified-acc-empty manifest threshold).va-index

  vindex-entries : List KindText, Integer, Integer, VerifiedAcc, TrustManifest, Integer -> VerifiedAcc
  vindex-entries (entries) (i) (len) (acc) (manifest) (threshold) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let e = list-at entries i
    in let acc2 = if e.kt-kind == persist-kind-tombstone
       then vindex-tomb acc (e.kt-text) manifest threshold
       else vindex-def acc (e.kt-text) manifest threshold
    in vindex-entries entries (i + 1) len acc2 manifest threshold

 A verified definition always enters the history; it takes the tree only if it
 is the newest thing said about its path.

  vindex-def : VerifiedAcc, Text, TrustManifest, Integer -> VerifiedAcc
  vindex-def (acc) (text) (manifest) (threshold) =
    when source-def-from-text text
      is None -> acc
      is Just (d) ->
        if def-verify d manifest threshold
        then let ix1 = index-works-add (acc.va-index) d
          in if stamp-wins (acc.va-stamp) (d.sd-path) (d.sd-timestamp)
             then VerifiedAcc { va-index = index-tree-set ix1 d, va-stamp = hamt-set (acc.va-stamp) (d.sd-path) (d.sd-timestamp) }
             else VerifiedAcc { va-index = ix1, va-stamp = acc.va-stamp }
        else acc

  vindex-tomb : VerifiedAcc, Text, TrustManifest, Integer -> VerifiedAcc
  vindex-tomb (acc) (text) (manifest) (threshold) =
    let t = tombstone-from-text text
    in if (tombstone-verify t manifest threshold) & (stamp-wins (acc.va-stamp) (t.tomb-path) (t.tomb-timestamp))
       then VerifiedAcc { va-index = index-remove (acc.va-index) (t.tomb-path), va-stamp = hamt-set (acc.va-stamp) (t.tomb-path) (t.tomb-timestamp) }
       else acc

Section: Signing A Definition

 This used to live in SourceFactsBridge and sign "hash|path|quire|chapter" --
 a sentence of its own invention, which no verifier in the tree knows how to
 check. The import gate verifies fact-signing-content: the content hash, the
 kind, the author and the timestamp. So that is what gets signed, and a
 definition signed here is one the gate can admit.

 The signature covers sd-hash, and sd-hash must address sd-content, or the
 gate's integrity guard refuses the fact before it ever looks at the
 signature. So the hash is recomputed here from the bytes we are about to
 store. A hash handed to us from outside is a claim about a file we did not
 read.

  sign-definition : Keypair, SourceDefinition, Integer -> SourceDefinition
  sign-definition (kp) (def) (timestamp) =
    let stamped = SourceDefinition {
      sd-hash = fact-content-hash (def.sd-content),
      sd-path = def.sd-path,
      sd-quire = def.sd-quire,
      sd-chapter = def.sd-chapter,
      sd-signer = kp.fingerprint,
      sd-signature = [],
      sd-author = kp.fingerprint,
      sd-timestamp = timestamp,
      sd-content = def.sd-content
    }
    in let sig = sign-content kp (fact-signing-content (source-def-fact stamped))
    in SourceDefinition {
      sd-hash = stamped.sd-hash,
      sd-path = stamped.sd-path,
      sd-quire = stamped.sd-quire,
      sd-chapter = stamped.sd-chapter,
      sd-signer = stamped.sd-signer,
      sd-signature = sig,
      sd-author = stamped.sd-author,
      sd-timestamp = stamped.sd-timestamp,
      sd-content = stamped.sd-content
    }

  repo-persist-save-definition : DiskFactStore, SourceDefinition, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  repo-persist-save-definition (store) (def) (timestamp) =
    when source-def-to-text def
    is Just (line) -> app-write-and-checkpoint store persist-kind-definition line timestamp
    is None -> store

 Retire a path. The tombstone is a fact like any other -- appended, never
 overwriting -- whose content is the path it removes; the index reads it in
 log order and drops the path from the current tree.

  repo-persist-save-tombstone : DiskFactStore, Text, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  repo-persist-save-tombstone (store) (path) (timestamp) =
    app-write-and-checkpoint store persist-kind-tombstone path timestamp

  repo-persist-load-last-definition : DiskFactStore -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  repo-persist-load-last-definition (store) =
    let found = app-scan-last store persist-kind-definition
    in when found
      is None -> None
      is Just (txt) -> source-def-from-text txt

  repo-persist-load-all-definitions : DiskFactStore -> List SourceDefinition
  repo-persist-load-all-definitions (store) =
    let texts = app-scan-all store persist-kind-definition
    in parse-source-def-list texts 0 (list-length texts) []

  parse-source-def-list : List Text, Integer, Integer, List SourceDefinition -> List SourceDefinition
  parse-source-def-list (texts) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let parsed = source-def-from-text (list-at texts i)
    in when parsed
      is Just (d) -> parse-source-def-list texts (i + 1) len (list-push acc d)
      is None -> parse-source-def-list texts (i + 1) len acc

Section: Proof Persistence (Kind 31)

 Format: hash|claim|author|timestamp|evidence

  repo-proof-to-text : RepoProof -> Text
  repo-proof-to-text (p) =
    p.rp-hash & "|" & p.rp-claim & "|"
    & p.rp-author & "|" & show p.rp-timestamp & "|"
    & p.rp-evidence

  repo-proof-from-text : Text -> Maybe RepoProof
  repo-proof-from-text (line) =
    let parts = text-split line "|"
    in if list-length parts < 5 then None
    else let pf = RepoProof {
      rp-hash = list-at parts 0,
      rp-claim = list-at parts 1,
      rp-evidence = list-at parts 4,
      rp-author = list-at parts 2,
      rp-timestamp = parse-decimal-full (list-at parts 3)
    }
    in Just pf

  repo-persist-save-proof : DiskFactStore, RepoProof, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  repo-persist-save-proof (store) (pf) (timestamp) =
    app-write-and-checkpoint store persist-kind-proof (repo-proof-to-text pf) timestamp

  repo-persist-load-last-proof : DiskFactStore -> Maybe RepoProof
  repo-persist-load-last-proof (store) =
    let found = app-scan-last store persist-kind-proof
    in when found
      is None -> None
      is Just (txt) -> repo-proof-from-text txt

  repo-persist-load-all-proofs : DiskFactStore -> List RepoProof
  repo-persist-load-all-proofs (store) =
    let texts = app-scan-all store persist-kind-proof
    in parse-proof-list texts 0 (list-length texts) []

  parse-proof-list : List Text, Integer, Integer, List RepoProof -> List RepoProof
  parse-proof-list (texts) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let parsed = repo-proof-from-text (list-at texts i)
    in when parsed
      is Just (p) -> parse-proof-list texts (i + 1) len (list-push acc p)
      is None -> parse-proof-list texts (i + 1) len acc

